Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Period live-action, Milwaukee, late 1990s. A cramped fifth-floor apartment holds MA'S pill bottles, an afghan, and old furniture. NICK, broad and scarred, reddish-brown hair uncombed, carries grocery bags in both fists.

(SOUND: stairwell footsteps and plastic grocery bags creaking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His face looked like a puzzle held together with sticky stuff from a glue gun.

NICK drops the bags. The NARRATOR, younger, lean, dark-haired, holds two smaller bags and watches Nick.

NICK
You got the rest, Bro?

NARRATOR
Yeah.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
After Dad took off, I lived with Nick and Ma. Nick made sure people moved out of the way.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Twelve-year-old NICK stands before MRS. ENGEL'S shattered kitchen window. Young PETER and MARK CONNER freeze beside dented garbage-can-lid bases.

(SOUND: broken glass settling onto linoleum)

MRS. ENGEL glares through the broken window.

MRS. ENGEL
Who kicked that ball through my kitchen?

NICK
I did it.

MARK stares at Nick, stunned.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Little Mark Conner kicked it. Nick stepped right up and said it was him.

(SOUND: a kickball rolls slowly through the alley)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A week later, Nick gave Ma cash for the window. He never told her the truth.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

NICK, now grown, in a black sleeveless shirt and work boots, approaches a curl bench. KEVIN MCCLEARY abandons a barbell and steps back.

(SOUND: iron weights clanging against the rubber floor)

NICK takes a heavier bar and begins curling it without strain. PETER watches from the edge of the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was a real-life Viking. Tall, strong, made for winter.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew he would stand up for me. I just didn’t know what that would cost.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

PETER wheels his bike toward the pizza parlor. SIONNA, pale and slim, with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a green backpack, black V-neck shirt, jeans, and blue sneakers, trips on uneven cement and brushes into him.

(SOUND: bicycle chain clicking and a city bus braking)

PETER catches her elbow.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

SIONNA brushes dust from her shirt. A small fish symbol hangs from her necklace.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That’s different.

SIONNA
My parents named me for an Irish goddess.

PETER looks into her eyes and forgets the pizza parlor door behind him.

PETER
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza.

SIONNA
But if you want a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

(SOUND: a gull cries above the traffic)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No girl had ever been interested in me. She was pretty, not cute. Intense.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA, OLIVIA OLSEN, an aging woman with soft brown hair and tired brown eyes, searches the kitchen counter. Pill bottles stand in a careful line.

(SOUND: television game-show applause from the living room)

MA
I can’t find my pain pills.

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER points to the pill organizer directly before her.

PETER
Your pills are right in front of you, Ma.

MA looks at him, glassy-eyed, then abruptly sees the bottles.

MA
Oh, right. I see them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like she didn’t know who I was.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK and PETER sit beneath a Packers game flickering on a television. Nick’s scarred face tightens as PETER describes MA'S confusion.

(SOUND: football crowd noise and pool balls cracking)

NICK
Why don’t you take her to the store or something?

NICK
Do something before she turns into some demented old fart.

PETER lowers his beer.

PETER
I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK leans close, fists hard on the bar.

NICK
You got responsibilities, Bro.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sometimes I really hated Nick inside.

EXT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

PETER crosses the cold street toward an old stone library. Tall windows glow over worn wooden floors and long rows of books.

(SOUND: winter wind rattling the library doors)

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

A LIBRARIAN gives PETER a temporary paper card. He wanders into CLASSIC LITERATURE and removes WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

(SOUND: pages turning and distant clock ticking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything was right here, in this building?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Students with backpacks crowd small tables. SIONNA sits beneath a brick wall in a loose green hooded top. PETER approaches with WUTHERING HEIGHTS tucked beneath his arm.

(SOUND: espresso machine hissing and ceramic cups clinking)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. It’s about a guy and girl who grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
The moors are like the people. Wild, unpredictable, infertile.

SIONNA
Heathcliff’s love for Catherine never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

PETER studies her, absorbing every word.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

EXT. CITY PARK - DAY

PETER and SIONNA walk beneath autumn trees. SIONNA carries a yoga mat. PETER holds a library book about Buddhism.

(SOUND: leaves scraping along the sidewalk)

SIONNA
Everything is changing. We have to be willing to let go.

PETER
Maybe I was dreaming about something from a past life.

SIONNA smiles.

SIONNA
Maybe.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

PETER struggles on a purple mat among silent students. SIONNA moves fluidly beside him. He collapses into corpse pose, shirt untucked from his waistband.

(SOUND: slow synchronized breathing and floorboards creaking)

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice.

PETER
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

PETER laughs quietly, then lies still.

(MUSIC: a soft sustained drone fading into silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For once, I didn’t feel violent, mad, or afraid. Just calm.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK stomps through the living room in a brown suede jacket and heavy boots. MA sits under her afghan. PETER stands before Nick, freshly home from yoga.

(SOUND: boots striking the wood floor and a game-show host shouting faintly)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass.

PETER
I totally forgot. I’m sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With who?

PETER
I met a girl.

NICK’s mouth tightens. He steps closer.

NICK
Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

PETER
I said I was sorry.

NICK slams the apartment door as he leaves.

(SOUND: door slam reverberating through the apartment)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was starting to think I had a life that belonged to me.

EXT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK, PETER, BOB, and SAMMY enter Joe’s packed bar. Rock music pounds. LINDSEY stands near a pool table with her new boyfriend, MARTIN.

(SOUND: hard rock music, glasses clinking, pool balls cracking)

NICK
Who’s she with?

PETER
Why does it matter? You broke up with her.

NICK ignores him and advances on LINDSEY and MARTIN.

NICK
Where did you meet this one?

MARTIN steps between Nick and Lindsey.

MARTIN
What the fuck is wrong with you?

NICK’S fists clench. The room falls watchful and still.

(SOUND: music continues under a sudden hush)

PETER steps directly before Nick.

PETER
Nick, leave it. You don’t even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it.

PETER
No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK swings. PETER jerks back as JOE and his bouncer seize Nick’s arms.

(SOUND: a fist whooshes past, followed by chairs scraping)

LINDSEY
Stop!

PETER runs from the bar.

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

PETER races up the porch steps, breath smoking in the cold. SIONNA opens the door and draws him inside.

(SOUND: PETER'S ragged breathing and a distant siren)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

SIONNA sits PETER on her green suede sofa. Her gray cat slips through the hallway.

(SOUND: a wall clock ticking steadily)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

PETER
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA touches his bruised cheek.

SIONNA
No one does that who really cares about you.

PETER
I love him. He’s my brother.

SIONNA
You can love someone and still not let him rule your life.

PETER rests his head in her lap.

PETER
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

SIONNA helps MA sort pills into a blue weekly organizer. PETER watches with a notebook and college forms. NICK enters, scarred face flushed, boots heavy, keys in hand.

(SOUND: pill bottles tapping into plastic compartments)

MA
You’re such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me.

NICK looks at the organizer.

NICK
Holy shit. Aren’t you Nurse Nancy?

PETER
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK smirks at SIONNA.

NICK
So, I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA
That’s right.

NICK
What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I’m thinking college professor.

NICK
Sometimes all that school just doesn’t pay off.

SIONNA sets her plate down carefully.

SIONNA
You can’t keep a good woman down.

(SOUND: forks lightly scraping china in the sudden silence)

NICK grips PETER’S shirt as he leaves.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

PETER
You know I will.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment is silent. MA’S chair is empty. PETER enters carrying books, notices the blinking telephone light, and presses the message button.

(SOUND: answering machine beep, then a recorded voice)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olsen, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
We need to talk to you regarding your family.

PETER grips the counter.

(SOUND: PETER'S breath quickening)

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, a broad middle-aged man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sits across from PETER at a metal desk.

(SOUND: phones ringing and fluorescent lights buzzing)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother and mother were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe.

PETER stares at the officer.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your mother passed away at the scene.

PETER’S paper cup crushes in his shaking hand.

(SOUND: water spilling from the crushed Styrofoam cup)

PETER
Ma is dead?

OFFICER DUPUIS nods.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - NIGHT

NICK lies unconscious beneath tubes and monitors. His wild reddish-brown hair sticks above bandages. PETER stands outside the doorway, unable to enter.

(SOUND: monitor beeps and ventilator hiss)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Pontiac was totaled. They pulled Ma out with the jaws of life.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The cops said toxicology was pending. They thought Nick may have been drunk.

PETER turns away from Nick’s room.

(SOUND: a monitor alarm briefly rises, then settles)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

SIONNA prepares food in the kitchen. Her brown hair is pinned up. PETER enters through the front door, carrying flowers from MA’S funeral.

(SOUND: knife chopping vegetables and a kettle beginning to whistle)

SIONNA turns. NICK suddenly appears in the doorway behind PETER, thinner and limping, a dark coat hanging from his broad frame.

NICK
I want to talk to you.

SIONNA
Nick, we can talk rationally.

PETER crosses toward them. NICK shoves SIONNA against the hall wall and clamps a hand around her throat.

(SOUND: SIONNA'S strained gasp and the kettle’s piercing whistle)

NICK flicks open a switchblade.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight.

PETER
Nick! What the fuck are you doing?

PETER grabs NICK’S arm. The blade plunges into SIONNA’S upper chest. Blood blooms across her shirt.

(SOUND: a sharp impact, followed by SIONNA collapsing)

NICK elbows PETER aside and flees.

PETER drops to SIONNA, presses both hands against the wound, and reaches for the phone.

PETER
Stay with me, Sionna. Stay with me.

(SOUND: 911 dialing tones, then sirens growing closer)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My brother stabbed her. He stabbed her.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

PETER stands at MA’S fresh grave beneath a gray late-autumn sky. He places golden mums into the small metal vase.

(SOUND: wind moving through bare branches)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mother: Olivia Anne Olsen. She had fought the good fight.

PETER kneels, tears falling onto the new grass.

PETER
You did keep the faith, Ma.

(SOUND: distant church bells tolling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick went to prison for killing Ma and trying to kill Sionna.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later, SIONNA sits rigidly in a rocking chair, a foam brace supporting her shoulder. Her eyes no longer meet PETER’S.

(SOUND: rocking chair creaking and rain tapping the windows)

PETER
You know I love you, right?

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

PETER kneels before her.

PETER
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you.

SIONNA looks toward the bedroom.

SIONNA
Maybe I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

(SOUND: the rocking chair stops)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My fingers slipped off the rocky cliff. I had no one now.

EXT. SEATTLE PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Years later. PETER, older, leaner, neatly dressed, walks through Pike Place Market with a confident stride. A sign for TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA hangs beneath a medical office building.

(SOUND: market vendors calling, ferry horns, and rain on awnings)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Years passed. Tony helped me get started, and I built my own chain in Seattle.

PETER pauses as a woman with long brown hair passes him. He closes his eyes for a beat.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I missed Sionna. I missed her smile, her smell, her kiss.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

PETER sits cross-legged on a mat among candles. His orange-and-white cat, BOZO, waits in the apartment beyond the studio windows.

(MUSIC: low bowls ringing beneath slow breaths)

INSTRUCTOR
Think of something you are grateful for.

PETER’S eyes remain closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I felt like I finally had the key to unlock it.

(SOUND: a single meditation bell rings)

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

PETER crosses a gravel lot toward an oppressive brick prison. He carries no bag, only a folded boarding pass in his pocket.

(SOUND: wind across the parking lot and a distant metal gate clanging)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had not seen Nick since his sentencing. I had to put it behind me forever.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISITATION ROOM - DAY

PETER sits at a Plexiglas partition and lifts a yellow telephone receiver. NICK enters on the far side in an orange prison uniform. His hair has thinned and grayed; his scarred face is lined, his once-massive shoulders slumped.

(SOUND: locked doors buzzing and phones murmuring)

NICK lifts his receiver.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

PETER
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
It’s not a cakewalk. I got my routine down.

PETER looks directly at him.

PETER
I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me.

NICK lowers his eyes.

PETER
You killed my mother in a drunk driving accident.

PETER
You held me down my entire life.

PETER
Mostly, you tried to kill the love of my life.

(SOUND: a guard’s keys jangle as he passes)

PETER’S grip loosens around the receiver.

PETER
I forgive you.

NICK looks up, stunned.

PETER
I forgive you because it sets me free.

NICK’S tears run down his face.

NICK
I’m so sorry.

NICK
You’re right. I did all that shit.

PETER
I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What are you doing now?

PETER allows himself a small smile.

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’ll be expanding.

PETER
You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK
I’m proud of you, man.

(SOUND: an intercom buzzes, signaling the visit’s end)

GUARD HARRIS
Time is up.

NICK
Bye.

PETER hangs up the receiver and watches Nick disappear through the locked door.

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

PETER walks toward the boarding line with a bag of mixed nuts and tea. Sun breaks briefly through clouds beyond the terminal glass.

(SOUND: boarding announcements, rolling suitcases, and aircraft engines)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The weight of thirty years was gone.

PETER looks down at his boarding pass.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

PETER smiles to himself.

PETER
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

(MUSIC: warm, restrained piano rising)

FADE OUT.

THE END
